WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:08.584
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.884 --> 00:00:16.737
Unit thirty-eight: The Self in Philosophy. In this unit, you will paraphrase and summarise competing ideas of the self.

4
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:18.728
selfhood.

5
00:00:19.428 --> 00:00:25.444
selfhood means the quality of being an individual person with a unique identity.

6
00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:28.545
Adolescence is a time when selfhood takes shape.

7
00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:32.002
Your turn. Say: selfhood.

8
00:00:34.702 --> 00:00:35.703
selfhood.

9
00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:38.136
existentialism.

10
00:00:38.836 --> 00:00:45.537
existentialism means a philosophy stressing freedom, choice and responsibility for creating meaning.

11
00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:49.195
Existentialism says we are defined by our choices.

12
00:00:49.895 --> 00:00:53.337
Your turn. Say: existentialism.

13
00:00:56.037 --> 00:00:57.398
existentialism.

14
00:00:58.383 --> 00:00:59.396
bad faith.

15
00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:05.462
bad faith means Sartre's term for denying our own freedom by pretending we have no choice.

16
00:01:05.762 --> 00:01:08.284
Saying 'I had no choice' can be bad faith.

17
00:01:08.984 --> 00:01:11.857
Your turn. Say: bad faith.

18
00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:15.651
bad faith.

19
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:17.988
self-knowledge.

20
00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:23.578
self-knowledge means understanding of one's own character, feelings and motives.

21
00:01:23.878 --> 00:01:27.770
'Know thyself': self-knowledge is the beginning of wisdom.

22
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.633
Your turn. Say: self-knowledge.

23
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:35.996
self-knowledge.

24
00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:38.286
narrative self.

25
00:01:38.986 --> 00:01:45.270
narrative self means the idea that we build our identity by telling stories about our lives.

26
00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:49.148
Your narrative self links your childhood to your future plans.

27
00:01:49.848 --> 00:01:52.617
Your turn. Say: narrative self.

28
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:56.643
narrative self.

29
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.139
embodied cognition.

30
00:01:59.839 --> 00:02:05.495
embodied cognition means the theory that thinking depends on the body, not just the brain.

31
00:02:05.795 --> 00:02:09.188
Embodied cognition explains why walking can help us think.

32
00:02:09.888 --> 00:02:13.818
Your turn. Say: embodied cognition.

33
00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:17.937
embodied cognition.

34
00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.590
mindfulness tradition.

35
00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:27.329
mindfulness tradition means Buddhist practices of calm, non-judgemental awareness of the present.

36
00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:32.346
The mindfulness tradition has spread from Asian monasteries to Western hospitals.

37
00:02:33.046 --> 00:02:36.279
Your turn. Say: mindfulness tradition.

38
00:02:38.979 --> 00:02:40.479
mindfulness tradition.

39
00:02:41.483 --> 00:02:42.763
impermanence.

40
00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:48.493
impermanence means the Buddhist teaching that everything changes and nothing lasts forever.

41
00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:51.860
Impermanence reminds us not to cling to possessions.

42
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.666
Your turn. Say: impermanence.

43
00:02:58.366 --> 00:02:59.587
impermanence.

44
00:03:00.583 --> 00:03:02.757
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:07.439
Ms. Sarah, Sartre and the Buddha both talk about the self. Do they agree?

46
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:15.463
Let me put it simply: Sartre says you create your self; the Buddha says there is no fixed self at all.

47
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.969
So, in other words, for Sartre we are totally free?

48
00:03:21.283 --> 00:03:26.142
Free and responsible. Denying that freedom is what he calls bad faith.

49
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.799
And Buddhism? I learned about impermanence at the pagoda.

50
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:36.869
In essence, everything changes, including the 'me' we cling to.

51
00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:43.225
To sum up, then: Sartre stresses choice; Buddhism stresses letting go.

52
00:03:44.517 --> 00:03:49.593
A neat summary, though both, in their way, demand honest self-knowledge.

53
00:03:50.883 --> 00:03:55.008
Grammar focus: Paraphrase and summary (extended).

54
00:03:55.308 --> 00:03:58.514
Paraphrase and summary show deep understanding.

55
00:03:58.814 --> 00:04:05.379
Let me put it simply: Sartre says you create your self. In essence, Buddhism says everything changes.

56
00:04:05.679 --> 00:04:10.198
To sum up, then: one stresses choice, the other letting go.

57
00:04:10.498 --> 00:04:17.479
A good paraphrase changes words and structure but keeps the meaning; a summary keeps only the main ideas.

58
00:04:18.483 --> 00:04:20.973
Now read and listen. Who Am I?.

59
00:04:21.273 --> 00:04:24.819
The question 'Who am I?' is among the oldest in philosophy.

60
00:04:26.117 --> 00:04:34.409
Socrates urged self-knowledge, while existentialism, in the twentieth century, placed freedom at the heart of selfhood.

61
00:04:35.717 --> 00:04:42.616
Jean-Paul Sartre argued that we are 'condemned to be free', and that pretending otherwise is bad faith.

62
00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:51.094
Buddhist thought, by contrast, teaches impermanence: no fixed, unchanging self can be found.

63
00:04:52.383 --> 00:04:58.075
The mindfulness tradition trains attention to observe thoughts and feelings as they arise and pass.

64
00:04:59.383 --> 00:05:05.516
Modern psychology adds the idea of a narrative self, an identity we construct through the stories we tell.

65
00:05:06.817 --> 00:05:13.425
Research on embodied cognition suggests that the self is shaped by the body as well as the mind.

66
00:05:14.717 --> 00:05:19.584
In essence, the self may be less a fixed object than an ongoing process.

67
00:05:20.883 --> 00:05:23.582
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:23.882 --> 00:05:28.146
Denying our own freedom by pretending we have no choice is blank.

69
00:05:28.446 --> 00:05:33.388
A, bad faith. B, selfhood. C, impermanence.

70
00:05:37.383 --> 00:05:39.778
The answer is A: bad faith.

71
00:05:41.283 --> 00:05:43.736
Which phrase introduces a paraphrase?

72
00:05:44.036 --> 00:05:48.641
A, In other words. B, Moreover. C, Despite this.

73
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.300
The answer is A: In other words.

74
00:05:56.783 --> 00:05:58.562
A summary should keep blank.

75
00:05:58.862 --> 00:06:04.280
A, only the main ideas. B, every detail. C, only examples.

76
00:06:08.283 --> 00:06:11.200
The answer is A: only the main ideas.

77
00:06:12.717 --> 00:06:16.098
The Buddhist teaching that nothing lasts forever is blank.

78
00:06:16.398 --> 00:06:22.640
A, impermanence. B, existentialism. C, embodied cognition.

79
00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.824
The answer is A: impermanence.

80
00:06:30.317 --> 00:06:35.358
According to the text, what does research on embodied cognition suggest?

81
00:06:35.658 --> 00:06:41.587
A, the body shapes the self. B, the self is fixed. C, the mind is separate from the body.

82
00:06:45.583 --> 00:06:48.628
The answer is A: the body shapes the self.

83
00:06:50.117 --> 00:06:56.182
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is your 'self' something you discover or something you create?

84
00:07:01.482 --> 00:07:06.361
Part two: Paraphrase one philosopher's view, then summarise your own position.

85
00:07:12.661 --> 00:07:25.603
Let me put Sartre's view simply: we create ourselves through choices. Buddhism, in essence, says the self keeps changing. To sum up, I think we discover our values and then create our self by acting on them.

86
00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:28.483
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:29.483 --> 00:07:34.841
Great work today! You learned eight new words and paraphrase and summary (extended).

88
00:07:35.141 --> 00:07:39.442
Today's value is self-acceptance. Big or little, you are special.

89
00:07:39.742 --> 00:07:43.787
See you in Unit thirty-nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:44.087 --> 00:07:46.531
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

